ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

QUICK START (WORKING SOLUTION)

Install required tools via package manager (recommended):

    $ sudo apt-get install gcc-riscv64-unknown-elf binutils-riscv64-unknown-elf qemu-system-misc

Verify installation:

    $ riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc --version
    $ qemu-system-riscv64 --version

Build xv6:

    $ make

This generates:
  - kernel/kernel: The compiled kernel binary
  - fs.img: The filesystem image with user programs

Run xv6 in QEMU:

    $ make qemu

The kernel will boot and you'll see a shell prompt. Try commands like:
  - ls     - list files
  - cat    - display file contents
  - echo   - print text
  - mkdir  - create directories
  - exit   - quit xv6

To exit QEMU, press Ctrl-A followed by X.

Debug with GDB:

    $ make qemu-gdb

In another terminal, connect GDB:

    $ riscv64-unknown-elf-gdb kernel/kernel
    (gdb) target remote localhost:25000
    (gdb) b main
    (gdb) c

Clean build artifacts:

    $ make clean

BUILDING TOOLCHAIN FROM SOURCE

If you prefer to build the toolchain from source instead of using packages:

    $ git clone https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain
    $ cd riscv-gnu-toolchain
    $ ./configure --prefix=/opt/riscv
    $ make -j$(nproc)
    $ export PATH=/opt/riscv/bin:$PATH

Note: Building from source requires network access and takes 20-40 minutes.
The pre-built package installation is significantly faster.
